INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7607,'This PR in SI should be reviewed as a first step.\n\nhttps://github.com/spring-projects/spring-integration/pull/875\n\nThe usecase in mind was to be able to do something with a filter such as \n\ns1 = twittersearch --query=\"#bieber\" | file\n\ntap:stream:s1 > json-path-filter --expression=\"$.entities.hashtag[*].text | field-value-counter\n\nAnother, perhaps more direct, alternative would be to use #jsonPath(...) as a SpEL function inside the standard spel based filter...\n\ntap:stream:s1 > filter --expression=\"#jsonPath(\'$.entities.hashgas[*].text\" | field-value-counter.\n\nThis would not require a whole new set of filter/router components, so is probably preferred.  Also joins in well with the fact we will need to register many other functions with SpEL (at least for some workflow support cases).\n\n','\"This PR in SI should be reviewed as a first step.  https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-integration/pull/875  The usecase in mind was to be able to do something with a filter such as   s1 = twittersearch --query=\"\"#bieber\"\" | file  tap:stream:s1 > json-path-filter --expression=\"\"$.entities.hashtag[*].text | field-value-counter  Another, perhaps more direct, alternative would be to use #jsonPath(...) as a SpEL function inside the standard spel based filter...  tap:stream:s1 > filter --expression=\"\"#jsonPath(\'$.entities.hashgas[*].text\"\" | field-value-counter.  This would not require a whole new set of filter/router components, so is probably preferred.  Also joins in well with the fact we will need to register many other functions with SpEL (at least for some workflow support cases).  \"','','2013-09-18 10:03:08',72,2940);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7608,'submitted PR:\nhttps://github.com/spring-projects/spring-xd/pull/301\n','\"submitted PR: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-xd/pull/301 \"','','2013-09-19 19:57:28',110,2940);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7609,'https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-xd/commit/3fc0218f94fdaea47f5356c1de1fcfc013005c15','\"https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-xd/commit/3fc0218f94fdaea47f5356c1de1fcfc013005c15\"','','2013-09-20 14:43:48',110,2940);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7610,'So syntactically how did this finally turn out?  In the existing documentation we have json-field-extractor and json-field-filter.  Do these change to be simply \'extractor\' and \'filter\'?  \n\nAssuming a simple \"--expression\" tag, is jsonPath expression itself going to be within a #jsonPath() spel function or \'naked\'?\n\nIs there an #xPath equivalent?\n\n\n','\"So syntactically how did this finally turn out?  In the existing documentation we have json-field-extractor and json-field-filter.  Do these change to be simply \'extractor\' and \'filter\'?    Assuming a simple \"\"--expression\"\" tag, is jsonPath expression itself going to be within a #jsonPath() spel function or \'naked\'?  Is there an #xPath equivalent?   \"','','2013-09-30 18:14:31',242,2940);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7611,'I believe docs need to be updated.  you can use it inside any of the spel aware components, so filter, router, etc.  and you use the Spel function.  We talked about an #xpath equivalent, it is doable, but prob more work than it is worth ATM since everyone hates XML :).  It ends up really being an SI issue, we just register the function that exists in SI.','\"I believe docs need to be updated.  you can use it inside any of the spel aware components, so filter, router, etc.  and you use the Spel function.  We talked about an #xpath equivalent, it is doable, but prob more work than it is worth ATM since everyone hates XML :).  It ends up really being an SI issue, we just register the function that exists in SI.\"','','2013-09-30 19:30:04',72,2940);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7612,'created https://jira.springsource.org/browse/XD-914','\"created https://jira.springsource.org/browse/XD-914\"','','2013-09-30 19:34:54',72,2940);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7613,'Regarding the \'everyone hates XML\' - that\'s true, but everyone has to deal with it :-)  I think adoption of XD will go down smoother if it can handle XML payloads easily.  Otherwise folks will give up and use SI, Camel or Mule.','\"Regarding the \'everyone hates XML\' - that\'s true, but everyone has to deal with it :-)  I think adoption of XD will go down smoother if it can handle XML payloads easily.  Otherwise folks will give up and use SI, Camel or Mule.\"','','2013-10-04 08:04:57',242,2940);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7615,'Implementation suggestion.\n\nFour endpoints for a get request\n\n/modules\n/modules/sources\n/modules/processors\n/modules/sinks\n\nThese would return a collection of \'ModuleDescription\' data.  This can start with\n\nname\ntype,\nrequired parameters,\noptional parameters\n\n\nThere should be Spring MVC style tests to validate the functionality.\n\n','\"Implementation suggestion.  Four endpoints for a get request  /modules /modules/sources /modules/processors /modules/sinks  These would return a collection of \'ModuleDescription\' data.  This can start with  name type, required parameters, optional parameters   There should be Spring MVC style tests to validate the functionality.  \"','','2013-09-18 08:53:53',72,2942);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7616,'https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-xd/commit/fa5853df86bd725f030755c8840d0f40febcffd5','\"https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-xd/commit/fa5853df86bd725f030755c8840d0f40febcffd5\"','','2013-11-04 07:20:54',110,2944);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7632,'merged','\"merged\"','','2013-09-27 05:26:22',94,2953);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7654,'submitted PR:\nhttps://github.com/spring-projects/spring-xd/pull/295\n\nWhile going through this code, I had some other ideas for refactoring the MessageBus implementations, but for now this is at least an improvement in the naming (should be more intuitive). We can open additional stories for other refactoring (e.g. to move more logic about bridging to the superclass), but that is not urgent.','\"submitted PR: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-xd/pull/295  While going through this code, I had some other ideas for refactoring the MessageBus implementations, but for now this is at least an improvement in the naming (should be more intuitive). We can open additional stories for other refactoring (e.g. to move more logic about bridging to the superclass), but that is not urgent.\"','','2013-09-18 08:07:53',110,2972);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (7667,'https://github.com/SpringSource/spring-xd/pull/281\n\nThe basics. There would be many things to follow from this:\n- shell commands (or command options) for defining these things rather than subverting the existing commands.\n- IIRC it doesn\'t support named channels yet (for sources/sinks).\n- Due to (for some reason, needs looking into) the 100 times the parser is called in various places, you must delete streams in the right order or you will get errors. So if you define A and then use it in constructing B, you need to delete B first or you will have problems if you remove A then attempt to remove B (an error)\n- When some stream is exploiting a composed module it needs to remember that - for later use when colocating the composed modules it picked up. (and possibly to help avoid the deletion problem above).  Also it would facilitate dynamic redeployment of all users of a composed module if that composed module definition is updated.\n- The parser has a syntax for parameterization of modules ${var:defaultValue} - need to test how this behaves with the spring property replacement\n\nPlan was to put this into an XD branch I think...','\"https://github.com/SpringSource/spring-xd/pull/281  The basics. There would be many things to follow from this: - shell commands (or command options) for defining these things rather than subverting the existing commands. - IIRC it doesn\'t support named channels yet (for sources/sinks). - Due to (for some reason, needs looking into) the 100 times the parser is called in various places, you must delete streams in the right order or you will get errors. So if you define A and then use it in constructing B, you need to delete B first or you will have problems if you remove A then attempt to remove B (an error) - When some stream is exploiting a composed module it needs to remember that - for later use when colocating the composed modules it picked up. (and possibly to help avoid the deletion problem above).  Also it would facilitate dynamic redeployment of all users of a composed module if that composed module definition is updated. - The parser has a syntax for parameterization of modules ${var:defaultValue} - need to test how this behaves with the spring property replacement  Plan was to put this into an XD branch I think...\"','','2013-09-06 12:59:16',103,2984);
