INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1334833,'Is there anyone or any team out there we can pay to add this functionality? It seems as though it\'s clearly not going to be worked on (as you said @rene, it\'s been 1.5 years). Maybe we should start a Gofundme page and hopefully hire someone with more Android technical know-how to implement this :)','\"Is there anyone or any team out there we can pay to add this functionality? It seems as though it\'s clearly not going to be worked on (as you said @rene, it\'s been 1.5 years). Maybe we should start a Gofundme page and hopefully hire someone with more Android technical know-how to implement this :)\"','','2016-04-20 12:03:50',166715,409082);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1334834,'https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/8095','\"https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/8095\"','','2016-06-28 17:22:14',166009,409082);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1334835,'Updated comment in the PR to mention that the PR does work for anything that is above Android L.\r\n\r\nAnything below that requires a different implementation.\r\n\r\nAlso we need to note that there are some limitations as mentioned in the docs [https://developer.android.com/studio/build/multidex.html](https://developer.android.com/studio/build/multidex.html).\r\n{quote}\r\nThe installation of .dex files during startup onto a device\'s data partition is complex and can result in Application Not Responding (ANR) errors if the secondary dex files are large. In this case, you should apply code shrinking techniques with ProGuard to minimize the size of dex files and remove unused portions of code.\r\n\r\nApplications that use multidex may not start on devices that run versions of the platform earlier than Android 4.0 (API level 14) due to a Dalvik linearAlloc bug (Issue 22586). If you are targeting API levels earlier than 14, make sure to perform testing with these versions of the platform as your application can have issues at startup or when particular groups of classes are loaded. Code shrinking can reduce or possibly eliminate these potential issues.\r\n\r\nApplications using a multidex configuration that make very large memory allocation requests may crash during run time due to a Dalvik linearAlloc limit (Issue 78035). The allocation limit was increased in Android 4.0 (API level 14), but apps may still run into this limit on Android versions prior to Android 5.0 (API level 21).\r\n\r\nThere are complex requirements regarding what classes are needed in the primary dex file when executing in the Dalvik runtime. The Android build tooling updates handle the Android requirements, but it is possible that other included libraries have additional dependency requirements including the use of introspection or invocation of Java methods from native code. Some libraries may not be able to be used until the multidex build tools are updated to allow you to specify classes that must be included in the primary dex file.\r\n{quote}\r\n','\"Updated comment in the PR to mention that the PR does work for anything that is above Android L.    Anything below that requires a different implementation.    Also we need to note that there are some limitations as mentioned in the docs [https://developer.android.com/studio/build/multidex.html](https://developer.android.com/studio/build/multidex.html).  {quote}  The installation of .dex files during startup onto a device\'s data partition is complex and can result in Application Not Responding (ANR) errors if the secondary dex files are large. In this case, you should apply code shrinking techniques with ProGuard to minimize the size of dex files and remove unused portions of code.    Applications that use multidex may not start on devices that run versions of the platform earlier than Android 4.0 (API level 14) due to a Dalvik linearAlloc bug (Issue 22586). If you are targeting API levels earlier than 14, make sure to perform testing with these versions of the platform as your application can have issues at startup or when particular groups of classes are loaded. Code shrinking can reduce or possibly eliminate these potential issues.    Applications using a multidex configuration that make very large memory allocation requests may crash during run time due to a Dalvik linearAlloc limit (Issue 78035). The allocation limit was increased in Android 4.0 (API level 14), but apps may still run into this limit on Android versions prior to Android 5.0 (API level 21).    There are complex requirements regarding what classes are needed in the primary dex file when executing in the Dalvik runtime. The Android build tooling updates handle the Android requirements, but it is possible that other included libraries have additional dependency requirements including the use of introspection or invocation of Java methods from native code. Some libraries may not be able to be used until the multidex build tools are updated to allow you to specify classes that must be included in the primary dex file.  {quote}  \"','','2016-07-18 07:31:45',166526,409082);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1334836,'For test case, please add modules to the Titanium app in 5.4.0 till it fails with {{Too many method references: 68927; max is 65536.}}\r\n\r\nThen switch to test with 6.0.0. It should pass as multidex is in use.','\"For test case, please add modules to the Titanium app in 5.4.0 till it fails with {{Too many method references: 68927; max is 65536.}}    Then switch to test with 6.0.0. It should pass as multidex is in use.\"','','2016-08-04 03:38:20',166526,409082);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1334837,'Verified the fix.\r\n\r\nChecked by using https://github.com/ashcoding/ReachTheLimit module.\r\nIt fails with 5.5.1.GA but works fine with 6.0.0 which has the fix.\r\n\r\nClosing. Please reopen if you find the issue is reproducible after the fix.\r\n\r\nEnvironment:\r\nAppc Studio : 4.8.0.201609292239\r\nTi SDK :  6.0.0.v20161002235150\r\nTi CLI : 5.0.10\r\nAlloy : 1.9.2\r\nMAC El Capitan : 10.11.6\r\nAppc NPM : 4.2.8-7\r\nAppc CLI : 6.0.0-55\r\nNode: 4.4.4\r\nNexus 5 - Android 6.0.1','\"Verified the fix.    Checked by using https://github.com/ashcoding/ReachTheLimit module.  It fails with 5.5.1.GA but works fine with 6.0.0 which has the fix.    Closing. Please reopen if you find the issue is reproducible after the fix.    Environment:  Appc Studio : 4.8.0.201609292239  Ti SDK :  6.0.0.v20161002235150  Ti CLI : 5.0.10  Alloy : 1.9.2  MAC El Capitan : 10.11.6  Appc NPM : 4.2.8-7  Appc CLI : 6.0.0-55  Node: 4.4.4  Nexus 5 - Android 6.0.1\"','','2016-10-03 22:57:43',166012,409082);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1334838,'How do we achieve multi-dex support in titanium for android versions < 5.0?\r\n\r\nI tried changing the android application name, i.e.:\r\n    <application android:name=\"android.support.multidex.MultiDexApplication\" >\r\n\r\nas mentioned in: https://developer.android.com/studio/build/multidex.html#mdex-pre-l\r\n\r\nbut while trying to run the application, the app crashes with the error:\r\n\r\nE/AndroidRuntime(32659): java.lang.RuntimeException: Unable to start activity ComponentInfo{com.xyz/com.xyz.XYZActivity}: java.lang.ClassCastException: android.support.multidex.MultiDexApplication cannot be cast to org.<USER>titanium.TiApplication\r\n\r\nOr how can I override the attachBaseContext method on the Main application class? ','\"How do we achieve multi-dex support in titanium for android versions < 5.0?    I tried changing the android application name, i.e.:      <application android:name=\"\"android.support.multidex.MultiDexApplication\"\" >    as mentioned in: https://developer.android.com/studio/build/multidex.html#mdex-pre-l    but while trying to run the application, the app crashes with the error:    E/AndroidRuntime(32659): java.lang.RuntimeException: Unable to start activity ComponentInfo{com.xyz/com.xyz.XYZActivity}: java.lang.ClassCastException: android.support.multidex.MultiDexApplication cannot be cast to org.<USER>titanium.TiApplication    Or how can I override the attachBaseContext method on the Main application class? \"','','2017-12-07 03:17:23',166021,409082);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338961,'Moving this deprecation request to engineering for further evaluation and prioritization.','\"Moving this deprecation request to engineering for further evaluation and prioritization.\"','','2014-05-19 06:53:44',166591,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338962,'Doc changes are taken care of by these PRs:\r\nhttps://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/6452\r\nhttps://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/6451/','\"Doc changes are taken care of by these PRs:  https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/6452  https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/6451/\"','','2014-12-11 18:52:45',166865,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338963,'I\'m against removal in the sense that there\'s no way to create a new execution context in a cross-platform manner with this API gone. This would cause [triple|https://github.com/<USER>triple] not to work anymore, or at the very least break some functionality. If we are going to remove the only means of creating a new context, we should be replacing it with a more appropriate API. I agree that hanging a {{url}} property off of {{Window}} doesn\'t make sense, but we should have something along the lines of node.js\'s [vm|http://nodejs.org/api/vm.html] module in order to allow devs to manage their own execution contexts.','\"I\'m against removal in the sense that there\'s no way to create a new execution context in a cross-platform manner with this API gone. This would cause [triple|https://github.com/<USER>triple] not to work anymore, or at the very least break some functionality. If we are going to remove the only means of creating a new context, we should be replacing it with a more appropriate API. I agree that hanging a {{url}} property off of {{Window}} doesn\'t make sense, but we should have something along the lines of node.js\'s [vm|http://nodejs.org/api/vm.html] module in order to allow devs to manage their own execution contexts.\"','','2014-12-12 13:06:41',166813,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338964,'[<USER>https://jira.<USER>org/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=<USER> to clarify my reasoning (as the original author of this JIRA) and to somewhat agree with your end point...\r\n\r\nI want this gone as too many newbies are using and thinking this is the correct way to go.  We are setting ourselves up for a massive future issue without heading this off now.\r\n\r\nAs to execution contexts - those are a good reason for having a feature - just not this one due to the historic usage and far too many example apps reliant on its usage because they \'do\' rather than they \'should\'.\r\n\r\nSo I am very much in agreement that an execution context feature is requires - but must have no relationship to the window.url property as it is now.  This way those who know what they are doing can use the new method, those who do not can start down a path of Alloy/CommonJS and thinking for themselves.','\"[<USER>https://jira.<USER>org/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=<USER> to clarify my reasoning (as the original author of this JIRA) and to somewhat agree with your end point...    I want this gone as too many newbies are using and thinking this is the correct way to go.  We are setting ourselves up for a massive future issue without heading this off now.    As to execution contexts - those are a good reason for having a feature - just not this one due to the historic usage and far too many example apps reliant on its usage because they \'do\' rather than they \'should\'.    So I am very much in agreement that an execution context feature is requires - but must have no relationship to the window.url property as it is now.  This way those who know what they are doing can use the new method, those who do not can start down a path of Alloy/CommonJS and thinking for themselves.\"','','2014-12-12 13:37:42',166333,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338965,'So... dump {{Ti.UI.Window.url}} and add {{Ti.App.createContext}} right?','\"So... dump {{Ti.UI.Window.url}} and add {{Ti.App.createContext}} right?\"','','2014-12-12 13:57:33',166377,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338966,'[~<USER> Sounds like we\'re saying the same thing.\r\n\r\n[~<USER> I don\'t know what the exact API should be, but yes, something entirely separate from any UI element for sure.','\"[~<USER> Sounds like we\'re saying the same thing.    [~<USER> I don\'t know what the exact API should be, but yes, something entirely separate from any UI element for sure.\"','','2014-12-12 14:09:58',166813,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338967,'Agree the functionality shouldn\'t be removed - changed / re-positioned maybe if that makes sense.','\"Agree the functionality shouldn\'t be removed - changed / re-positioned maybe if that makes sense.\"','','2014-12-12 14:23:53',166753,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338968,'For those interested in the \"createContext\" feature, please provide a sample bit of code that demonstrates how you would use it properly.','\"For those interested in the \"\"createContext\"\" feature, please provide a sample bit of code that demonstrates how you would use it properly.\"','','2014-12-12 14:58:52',166152,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338969,'Here\'s the chunk of code from {{triple}} that relies on {{Window.url}}, but would be better served by a true context creation API:\r\n\r\n{code:javascript}\r\n// create a new execution context with createWindow\'s url property\r\nfunction resetContext() {\r\n	var hasCurrent = !!current;\r\n	if (hasCurrent) {\r\n		current.removeEventListener(\'close\', resetContext);\r\n		current.removeEventListener(\'open\', returnReset);\r\n		Ti.App.fireEvent(\'app:reset\');\r\n	}\r\n	current = Ti.UI.createWindow({\r\n		url: \'context.js\',\r\n		exitOnClose: false\r\n	});\r\n	current.addEventListener(\'close\', resetContext);\r\n	hasCurrent && current.addEventListener(\'open\', returnReset);\r\n	current.open();\r\n}\r\nresetContext();\r\n{code}\r\n\r\nInstead of all those window and application event hacks, it would be nice to just have an API that either clears the current context, or creates a new one. But, that\'s not entirely useful unless we have APIs for executing code inside a given context, like the vm module I mentioned above. So my proposal would be implementing the {{vm}} API from node.js in Titanium (it\'s small). Where it goes in the namespaces is not important to me, be it {{Ti.App}} or {{Ti.VM}} or whatever.','\"Here\'s the chunk of code from {{triple}} that relies on {{Window.url}}, but would be better served by a true context creation API:        Instead of all those window and application event hacks, it would be nice to just have an API that either clears the current context, or creates a new one. But, that\'s not entirely useful unless we have APIs for executing code inside a given context, like the vm module I mentioned above. So my proposal would be implementing the {{vm}} API from node.js in Titanium (it\'s small). Where it goes in the namespaces is not important to me, be it {{Ti.App}} or {{Ti.VM}} or whatever.\"','\"  // create a new execution context with createWindow\'s url property  function resetContext() {   var hasCurrent = !!current;   if (hasCurrent) {    current.removeEventListener(\'close\', resetContext);    current.removeEventListener(\'open\', returnReset);    Ti.App.fireEvent(\'app:reset\');   }   current = Ti.UI.createWindow({    url: \'context.js\',    exitOnClose: false   });   current.addEventListener(\'close\', resetContext);   hasCurrent && current.addEventListener(\'open\', returnReset);   current.open();  }  resetContext();  \"','2014-12-12 15:31:28',166813,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338970,'I\'m all for adding VM context support, but I\'m not sold that we should put it in the Titanium namespace. I strongly believe we should design this feature to be compatible with Node.js\'s VM API (http://nodejs.org/api/vm.html).\r\n\r\nI think it would be great if we could implement this in a Titanium Module. This gives us a clean Titanium-free way of require()\'ing the module (like node) and invoking the API.','\"I\'m all for adding VM context support, but I\'m not sold that we should put it in the Titanium namespace. I strongly believe we should design this feature to be compatible with Node.js\'s VM API (http://nodejs.org/api/vm.html).    I think it would be great if we could implement this in a Titanium Module. This gives us a clean Titanium-free way of require()\'ing the module (like node) and invoking the API.\"','','2014-12-12 18:38:58',166050,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338971,'[~<USER> I\'m totally down with that idea. I thought I\'d be the only one to propose such a radical deviation from the normal Titanium pattern, but I should have known better. Does anyone know if creating contexts is possible through the module system? On all platforms? Who can we include in this conversation to get some clarity?','\"[~<USER> I\'m totally down with that idea. I thought I\'d be the only one to propose such a radical deviation from the normal Titanium pattern, but I should have known better. Does anyone know if creating contexts is possible through the module system? On all platforms? Who can we include in this conversation to get some clarity?\"','','2014-12-15 01:38:52',166813,410024);
INSERT INTO `Comment` VALUES (1338972,'I would like to learn more about this sounds intresting I quite a new titanium developer so need all the helpi can get from the pro\'s','\"I would like to learn more about this sounds intresting I quite a new titanium developer so need all the helpi can get from the pro\'s\"','','2014-12-28 03:11:15',167206,410024);
